You must:

- be a U.S. citizen;
- be 17 years old (16 years old if registering in person at the registrar of voters office or at the Louisiana Office of Moter Vehicles), but must be 18 years old to vote;
- not be under an order of imprisonment for conviction of a felony;
- not be under a judgment of full interdiction for mental incompetence or partial interdiction with suspension of voting rights;
- reside in the state and parish in which you seek to register;
- must be registered at least 20 days prior to an election if registering through our GeauxVote Online Registration System (https://www.sos.la.gov:443/elections-voting/online-voter-registration) with a Louisiana driver's license or Louisiana special ID card or 30 days prior to an election if registering in person or by mail (https://www.sos.la.gov:443/media/emudgftt/application-to-register-to-vote.pdf) to be eligible to vote in that particular election. (If mailing in an application, the application or envelope must be postmarked 30 days prior to the first election in which you seek to vote.)

See the XIV Amendment of the U.S. Constitution  (https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs/amendments-11-27), the XXVI Amendment of the U.S. Constitution  (https://www.archives.gov/founding-docs/amendments-11-27), La R.S. 18:101  (http://www.legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=81242) and La R.S. 18:102  (http://www.legis.la.gov/Legis/Law.aspx?d=81243) for more information about voter registration qualifications.

Voter registration information on 16 and 17 year olds is confidential and not open to public inspection.

In Louisiana you must have a reason to be eligible to vote by mail. Reasons include:

- military or overseas citizens;
- senior citizens (65+);
- voters with disabilities;
- higher education students & professors;
- religious clergy;
- voters temporarily absent during early voting and election day;
- voters who moved to a new parish more than 100 miles from former residence within 30 days of election;
- persons involuntarily confined to institution;
- sequestered jurors;
- voters hospitalized or who expect to be hospitalized during early voting and election day;
- voters employed upon state waters;
- voters incarcerated for non-felony with sheriff's certification; and
- participants in the state's address confidentiality program.

If you registered to vote online or by mail and you have not previously voted in the parish, you are not eligible to vote absentee and must vote in person the first time, either during early voting or on election day. Exceptions include:

- military or overseas citizens;
- senior citizens (65+);
- people who are homebound because of a disability;
- students, who submit a copy of student ID or fee bill with the request;
- citizens who appear in the registrar of voters office before the election to verify identity;
- participants in the state's address confidentiality program; and
- a person who was registered to vote in another parish and previously voted in the other parish (R.S. 18:115(F)(e))

Voters, who have a physical disability or are unable to read, may request assistance in voting when they are registering to vote or when making a change to their voter registration.

The person providing assistance will fill out a Voter Assistance Form prior to assisting in voting. Voters who need assistance are allowed up to 20 minutes to cast their ballots and may choose to receive the assistance from any person, including a commissioner, with these exceptions:

- a candidate in the election;
- an employer or employer's agent;
- a union agent;
- staff of state-operated facilities for disabled persons (by rule, the Department of Health (LDH) prohibits staff from assisting residents of state-operated facilities for mentally disabled/developmentally disabled persons); and
- owners, operators, administrators or employees of a nursing home, veterans' home or long stay hospitals for those with physical disabilities may not assist a person in voting.

No person assisting a voter shall reveal the name of any person for whom the voter voted, any proposition upon which the person voted, or anything which took place while the voter was being assisted.

The registrar of voters must verify that you are qualified to vote by mail. If your eligibility cannot be verified, then you will be notified in writing and you will not receive a ballot. See Who can vote by mail? Some reasons your request to vote by mail may be rejected are:

- your request was not received timely (see What is the timeline to vote by mail?);
- you registered by mail, and you must vote in person the first time, unless an exception applies (see Who can vote by mail?);
- you applied to vote by mail from a parish or district/precinct where you are not registered to vote or eligible to vote;
- your application to register to vote was not received timely and therefore, you are not eligible to vote in the election (you must register to vote 30 days before an election in order to vote in the election); or
- your request was incomplete (you may still vote early or on Election Day).

When voting absentee, check to make sure you have received all of the following from the Registrar of Voters:

1. Official ballot(s) (white paper ballot(s) for either the primary or general election);
2. Secrecy Envelope (to seal your voted ballot);
3. Certificate Envelope (to seal your secrecy envelope and certify your information);
4. Return Envelope (to mail certificate envelope with voted ballot(s) inside the secrecy envelope to the Registrar of Voters); and
5. Instructions for absentee voting.

When voting absentee, check to make sure you have received all of the following from the Registrar of Voters:

1. Official white paper ballot for the primary election;
2. Green ranking ballot for the general election (run-offs if there are two (2) or more candidates in a race);
3. Secrecy Envelope (there will be two if a green ranking ballot is included);
4. Certificate Envelope (to seal your secrecy envelope and certify your information) (there will be two if a green ranking ballot is included);
5. Return Envelope (to mail certificate envelope with voted ballot(s) inside the secrecy envelope to the registrar of voters);
6. Instructions for absentee voting.

If there are any propositions added to a general election, you may receive a second mailing with a paper ballot for the general election propositions that were not on the green ranking ballot, along with a secrecy envelope, certificate envelope, return envelope, and instructions for absentee voting.

Voters who have a physical disability or are unable to read may request assistance in voting when they are registering to vote or when making a change to their voter registration.

The person providing assistance will fill out a Voter Assistance Form prior to assisting in voting. Voters who need assistance are allowed up to 20 minutes to cast their ballots and may choose to receive the assistance from any person, including a commissioner, with these exceptions:

- a candidate in the election;
- a commissioner-in-charge;
- an employer or employer's agent;
- a union agent;
- staff of state-operated facilities for disabled persons (by rule, the Department of Health (LDH) prohibits staff from assisting residents of state-operated facilities for mentally disabled/developmentally disabled persons); and
- owners, operators, administrators or employees of a nursing home, veterans' home or long stay hospitals for those with physical disabilities may not assist a person in voting.

No person assisting a voter shall reveal the name of any person for whom the voter voted, any proposition upon which the person voted or anything which took place while the voter was being assisted.

For the Nov. 5, 2024 General and Referendum Election, the deadline to request an absentee ballot including a ballot voted in the presence of the clerk, is Thursday, October 31, 2024, unless the voter completes a special circumstances application, stating one of four allowable reasons for requesting an absentee ballot after this deadline. The five reasons are:

- An unexpected absence from the municipality during the entire time the polls are open on election day;
- A physical or mental disability;
- An inability to travel to the polls if the voter is a resident of an island ward or precinct;
- An incapacity or illness that has resulted in the voter being unable to leave home or a treatment facility; or
- Being a caregiver who is unable to attend the polls due to the incapacity or illness of another person.

This special circumstances application must be signed by the voter. This application can be obtained from your municipal clerk or from the Secretary of State.

In order to register to vote, an individual must be:

1. A citizen of the United States;
2. A resident of the State of Missouri; and
3. 17 years and 6 months of age (must be 18 by Election Day).

If an individual meets these requirements, they are entitled to register to vote. To be entitled to vote, a person:

1. Must be registered to vote in the jurisdiction of the person's domicile prior to the election;
2. Cannot be imprisoned;
3. Cannot be on probation or parole after conviction of a felony pursuant to chapter 566 (https://revisor.mo.gov/main/OneChapter.aspx?chapter=566) or 573 (https://revisor.mo.gov/main/OneSection.aspx?section=573) or section 565.020 (https://revisor.mo.gov/main/OneSection.aspx?section=565.020), 565.021 (https://revisor.mo.gov/main/OneSection.aspx?section=565.021), 565.050 (https://revisor.mo.gov/main/OneSection.aspx?section=565.050), 565.052 (https://revisor.mo.gov/main/OneSection.aspx?section=565.052), 565.072 (https://revisor.mo.gov/main/OneSection.aspx?section=565.072), 565.079 (https://revisor.mo.gov/main/OneSection.aspx?section=565.079), 568.020 (https://revisor.mo.gov/main/OneSection.aspx?section=568.020), 568.045 (https://revisor.mo.gov/main/OneSection.aspx?section=568.045), or 569.160 (https://revisor.mo.gov/main/OneSection.aspx?section=569.160);
4. Cannot have been convicted of a felony or misdemeanor connected to voting; and
5. Cannot have been declared incapacitated.

